WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.303 --> 00:00:03.860
- A global icon celebrated
all over the world, check.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:06.510
Leader of a political revolution, check.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:10.250
Prolific composer for your
own genre of music, check.

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.810
Oh, and Beyonce thinks you're amazing?

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:13.870
Check.

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:16.630
What musician could
possibly be this incredible?

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:19.151
I'd like to invite you to
the world of Fela Kuti,

00:00:19.151 --> 00:00:21.829
and his music, Afrobeat.

00:00:21.829 --> 00:00:24.412
(upbeat music)

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:30.029
Trumpeter Miles Davis said Fela Kuti was

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:32.910
a life-transforming artist.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.477
Bassist Bootsy Collins
said of Fela's band,

00:00:35.477 --> 00:00:37.020
"We were telling them
they're the funkiest cats

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:38.840
we ever heard in our life.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:40.840
I mean, I play with James Brown's band,

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.110
but we were totally wiped out!"

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:45.693
(upbeat music)

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:52.220
Afrobeat roots itself in West
African rhythms and melody,

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:54.600
textually on African verbal art,

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.170
particularly Yoruba praise poetry,

00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:59.620
the harmonic backdrops of funk and soul,

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:01.710
and the improvised nature of jazz.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.849
What makes Afrobeat music so special?

00:01:03.849 --> 00:01:07.100
- Because of how much
it's drawn from, you know,

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.470
cultural sounds with traditional rhythms,

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:12.750
traditional chants, and the horns,

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.030
the arrangements are so
focused on groove and

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.722
rhythm, West African rhythm,
instrumental interplay,

00:01:17.722 --> 00:01:22.722
funk in the most cultural way
is what Afrobeat I think is.

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:24.480
- I like, that was really well put.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.820
Funk in the most cultural
way, that's true.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:27.730
I dig that.

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:28.630
I dig it.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:30.345
- Even if you aren't
familiar with Afrobeat,

00:01:30.345 --> 00:01:32.650
it's likely that you like music

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:34.190
that couldn't exist without it.

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:35.950
From Vibraphonist Roy Ayers,

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:38.044
who toured and recorded with Fela.

00:01:38.044 --> 00:01:40.627
(upbeat music)

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:44.970
Or even Michael Jackson and Rihanna,

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:47.380
borrowing vocal chants
from Cameroonian artist,

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:49.144
Manu Dibango.

00:01:49.144 --> 00:01:51.727
(upbeat music)

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:00.100
- How about Beyonce
loving Afrobeat so much,

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:03.270
she recorded an entire
Fela Kuti inspired album

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:04.770
that was never released.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:06.225
- Here's her song "End of Time",

00:02:06.225 --> 00:02:10.270
which she says has a baseline,
percussion, and horns

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:12.195
inspired by Fela's Afrobeat.

00:02:12.195 --> 00:02:14.778
(upbeat music)

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:19.136
- Recently, Nigeria's Burna
Boy, hit the global scene

00:02:19.136 --> 00:02:22.200
notching two back-to-back
Grammy nominations.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.421
He's called Fela Kuti
the greatest musician,

00:02:24.421 --> 00:02:27.701
and you can hear the influence
directly in his music.

00:02:27.701 --> 00:02:30.284
(upbeat music)

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:40.601
- What do you think, in your
words, what do you think makes

00:02:40.601 --> 00:02:44.860
Fela Kuti so inspirational to
musicians across the world?

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:48.173
- From his compositions,
and his bravery, and his,

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:50.803
his sincerity,

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:55.450
in never compromising,

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:56.630
never selling out.

00:02:56.630 --> 00:02:57.463
- Wow.

00:02:57.463 --> 00:03:01.500
- It was all things you
need be a great composer.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:06.070
So those ingredients are, is
the foundation of the Afrobeat.

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:08.465
- So now that we've heard all
about Fela Kuti's influence,

00:03:08.465 --> 00:03:11.100
let's take it back to the 1960's

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.110
to learn about his musical origins.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.980
By the sixties, the African
popular sounds that went on to

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:18.989
form Afrobeat, had been established
by musicians from Ghana,

00:03:18.989 --> 00:03:21.150
and other West African countries.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:24.498
The genre fused Yoruba
music, highlife, and jazz,

00:03:24.498 --> 00:03:26.880
and was brought to life
in Nigeria with the help

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:28.739
of saxophonist, Orlando Julius,

00:03:28.739 --> 00:03:32.108
and Fela Kuti's own band, Koola Lobitos.

00:03:32.108 --> 00:03:34.691
(upbeat music)

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:38.510
Tony Allen was a legendary drummer,

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:40.198
who played in Koola Lobitos.

00:03:40.198 --> 00:03:43.698
His unique style fused
American jazz with highlife,

00:03:43.698 --> 00:03:46.860
and Fela himself said, without Tony Allen,

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:48.782
there would be no Afrobeat.

00:03:48.782 --> 00:03:51.120
(upbeat music)

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:54.930
Then, in 1969, Fela Kuti and his band

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:56.260
went through an evolution.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:58.979
While living in Los
Angeles, Fela's group held

00:03:58.979 --> 00:04:02.430
down a nightly residency
at Citadel 'd Haiti,

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:05.030
a small club on Sunset Boulevard.

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:07.257
The band did well enough
to pack the club regularly,

00:04:07.257 --> 00:04:11.250
playing covers of popular soul
records and jazz standards.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.120
But according to Sandra Izsadore,

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:14.659
his girlfriend at the time,

00:04:14.659 --> 00:04:17.580
something was missing
from their performances.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:20.197
She pointed him toward a
different artistic direction.

00:04:20.197 --> 00:04:22.289
In an interview, Fela said,

00:04:22.289 --> 00:04:25.500
"Sandra gave me the
education I wanted to know.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:27.200
Sandra was my adviser.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.080
She talked to me about politics, history.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:31.440
She taught me what she knew,

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.400
and what she knew was
enough for me to start on."

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:36.440
This inspired Fela to use his talents

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:38.758
for the benefit of his
people, just like his mother,

00:04:38.758 --> 00:04:41.310
Funmilayo Ransome-Kuti,

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:44.100
who was a women's rights
activists back in Nigeria.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:45.770
Fela didn't have the proper permits

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:47.178
to stay in the United States.

00:04:47.178 --> 00:04:50.670
He recorded a live album
called "Fela Fela Fela"

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:52.440
before returning to Nigeria.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:54.580
This album signifies
the point right before

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:57.697
his band came into their
signature Afrobeat sound.

00:04:57.697 --> 00:05:00.280
(upbeat music)

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:05.100
And compare that sound, to "Lady",

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.539
which was recorded just three years later.

00:05:07.539 --> 00:05:10.122
(upbeat music)

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:14.360
Fela's evolution from club band leader,

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.940
to leading a musical and
political revolution with Afrobeat

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:19.380
didn't happen overnight.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:21.870
Returning to Nigeria
allowed his sound to grow

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:23.853
to the Fela Kuti remembered today.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:28.186
So you're probably wondering,
what is the Afrobeat sound?

00:05:28.186 --> 00:05:31.800
A large part of Afrobeat's
magic is how it weaves tradition

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.330
with music to create its own fabric.

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:38.720
Call and response, improvisation,
and short rhythmic phrases

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:39.970
are the common threads poured

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:42.070
from across the African diaspora.

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:44.697
While Afrobeat was influenced
by American genres,

00:05:44.697 --> 00:05:47.610
it also influenced them in return.

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:49.950
Listen for the tight
harmonies in the horns,

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:53.779
and the syncopated rhythm in
Fela's song, "Fight to Finish".

00:05:53.779 --> 00:05:56.362
(upbeat music)

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:01.460
Many Afrobeat songs use
a layering technique

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:03.210
by combining different rhythms together

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:04.690
to create the groove.

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:07.630
Most instruments are playing
a one or two bar phrase

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:09.010
at any given moment.

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:10.660
When layered on top of each other,

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:13.760
they create one wide sound
that's constantly moving.

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:16.870
Listen to the repetitive
patterns played by the guitar,

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:18.770
the horns, and the bass

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:21.926
in Fela Kuti's song, "Yellow Fever".

00:06:21.926 --> 00:06:24.509
(upbeat music)

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:29.108
The horns often play
the melody of the song,

00:06:29.108 --> 00:06:32.870
but are also used for call
and response with the vocals.

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:34.970
Listen to "Shuffering and Shmiling",

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:36.321
where the singers are the call,

00:06:36.321 --> 00:06:38.468
and the horns are the response.

00:06:38.468 --> 00:06:41.051
(upbeat music)

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:48.410
You'll also find the horns,
drums, and other instruments

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:51.000
going for long improvised solos.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:54.540
Because of this, Afrobeat songs are long,

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:58.320
anywhere from 15 minutes
to an entire hour.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:00.530
Listen to Fela improvise on the saxophone

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:02.983
in the song "Power Show".

00:07:02.983 --> 00:07:05.566
(upbeat music)

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:15.800
Fela Kuti's long songs are
often broken into two parts.

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:17.830
The first half consists of a jam

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:20.700
filled with instrumental
solos and no vocals.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:23.990
Then, about halfway through
the song, the vocals come in.

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:25.453
The lead vocal takes the helm,

00:07:25.453 --> 00:07:27.010
and the background vocals

00:07:27.010 --> 00:07:29.270
chime in with call and response chants

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:31.070
that reflect the energy of the lead.

00:07:32.222 --> 00:07:34.805
(upbeat music)

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:40.080
Afrobeat music often
incorporates political messages

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:41.460
into its lyrics.

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:45.144
Common themes are
anti-colonialism, pan-Africanism,

00:07:45.144 --> 00:07:47.190
and antiwar sentiment.

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:49.202
Putting a spotlight to the
current climate of Africa

00:07:49.202 --> 00:07:52.380
has been one of Afrobeat's
strongest superpowers.

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:55.985
For example, in "Zombie", Fela
sings about Nigerian soldiers

00:07:55.985 --> 00:07:58.010
mindlessly following the commands

00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:00.034
of their corrupt government.

00:08:00.034 --> 00:08:02.617
(upbeat music)

00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:09.593
What is one thing you
remember most about Fela Kuti?

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:14.230
- I mean, he could be funny, he was a,

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:16.150
when it comes to being on the stage

00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:18.154
he was a completely different person.

00:08:18.154 --> 00:08:18.987
- Oh.

00:08:18.987 --> 00:08:22.370
- He could stop the band in
the middle of a concert too.

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:26.232
If anybody played the
wrong note or was not, um.

00:08:26.232 --> 00:08:27.556
- In tune.

00:08:27.556 --> 00:08:31.200
- In tune, to restart the whole groove.

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:32.033
- Wow.

00:08:32.033 --> 00:08:33.427
- He wasn't a conventional father.

00:08:33.427 --> 00:08:35.077
- He wasn't at all.

00:08:35.077 --> 00:08:37.690
(laughter)

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:40.140
- After newfound freedom
from British Colonialism

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:41.480
and the civil war,

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:44.320
Nigeria was entering a
new phase of its history.

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:47.620
By the 1970's, the Federal
Military Government

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:49.920
assumed control of the country.

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:53.360
As a result of the civil war
that ended in January 1970,

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:55.360
people went hungry,

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:57.400
families lost their homes,

00:08:57.400 --> 00:08:59.975
and the after effects
of colonialism ran amuck

00:08:59.975 --> 00:09:02.160
with the military holding onto power

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:06.170
through October 1979,
albeit with other coups.

00:09:06.170 --> 00:09:08.780
Fela believed the people of
Africa could fight against

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:11.100
an unjust military regime.

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:12.746
He sought ways to couple his music

00:09:12.746 --> 00:09:14.980
with his newfound activism.

00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:17.956
This led to the renaming
of his band to "Africa 70"

00:09:17.956 --> 00:09:20.930
and the creation of the Kalakuta Republic.

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:24.180
Created in 1970, the Kalakuta Republic

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:27.000
was a commune he declared
an independent state.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:28.970
The Kalakuta Republic was home base

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:31.630
for almost all of his 1970's albums,

00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:34.920
including "Gentlemen", "Fear
Not For Man", and "Zombie".

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:37.197
All of the political
passion he held inside

00:09:37.197 --> 00:09:39.660
was baked into his music here,

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:42.870
free from the outside influence
of Nigerian government.

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:45.895
In his two part song,
"Confusion", he states it plainly,

00:09:45.895 --> 00:09:48.026
everything's out of control.

00:09:48.026 --> 00:09:50.609
(upbeat music)

00:09:55.143 --> 00:09:57.544
His ability to politically
influence his growing audience

00:09:57.544 --> 00:10:01.070
became a thorn in the
Nigerian government's foot.

00:10:01.070 --> 00:10:03.930
To slow down his momentum,
he was thrown into jail

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:06.100
over 200 times.

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:08.550
The Kalakuta Republic was a safe haven

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:11.250
to all who chose to fight
against the colonial mindset

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:13.310
plaguing Africa at the time.

00:10:13.310 --> 00:10:17.560
In 1977, the Nigerian army
burned down the commune

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:19.925
in a raid that resulted
in his mother's injury,

00:10:19.925 --> 00:10:22.990
from which she never
recovered until her death.

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:25.680
Fela Kuti's legacy is
in full effect today,

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:28.870
despite the absence of his
original stomping grounds.

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:31.470
The yearly Felabration
festival was created

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:33.464
by Fela's daughter, Yeni Kuti,

00:10:33.464 --> 00:10:35.970
and it honors the father of Afrobeat.

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:38.800
In New York city, local
music venue "Shrine"

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:41.977
is an American tribute to Fela's
original performance space,

00:10:41.977 --> 00:10:43.780
"Africa Shrine".

00:10:43.780 --> 00:10:46.110
Fela's children have
continued on his legacy

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:49.210
by evolving Afrobeat with
their own musical talents.

00:10:49.210 --> 00:10:52.780
His youngest son, Seun Kuti,
has taken on the lead role

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:56.470
in his father's band,
now called "Egypt 80".

00:10:56.470 --> 00:10:58.507
In their Grammy nominated
album, "Black Times",

00:10:58.507 --> 00:11:00.690
you can hear Afrobeat live on,

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:03.247
while Seun sings about
justice for his people.

00:11:03.247 --> 00:11:05.830
(upbeat music)

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:12.410
Fela's oldest son, Femi Kuti,
continues to pair activism

00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:15.310
with music, and has earned
four Grammy nominations

00:11:15.310 --> 00:11:16.143
of his own.

00:11:16.143 --> 00:11:19.507
Fun fact, in 2017, Femi broke the record

00:11:19.507 --> 00:11:22.690
for the longest note on
a woodwind instrument

00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:25.373
at 51 minutes and 35 seconds.

00:11:26.470 --> 00:11:29.553
(musical instrument)

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:35.820
This year, Femi teamed up
with his son, Made Kuti,

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:38.800
to release a joint album
called "Legacy Plus".

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:41.990
In it, Femi preaches about the
struggles of his community.

00:11:41.990 --> 00:11:44.573
(upbeat music)

00:11:48.350 --> 00:11:50.430
Made's music takes Afrobeat further

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:52.441
by blending it with
more influence from jazz

00:11:52.441 --> 00:11:55.590
and a psychedelic feeling
that calls back to Fela's

00:11:55.590 --> 00:11:57.680
1960's musical roots.

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:00.263
(upbeat music)

00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:08.270
What is the album "Legacy +"
about, and how do you intend

00:12:08.270 --> 00:12:11.060
people to feel when hearing the new album?

00:12:11.060 --> 00:12:13.560
- We show how passionate
we are about the sound,

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:15.380
how passionate we are about the message.

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:17.874
Nobody helped anybody
write anything and yet

00:12:17.874 --> 00:12:20.160
the music and the message is so similar.

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:21.984
It shows how finely in tune we are.

00:12:21.984 --> 00:12:24.901
And the legacy is very much, as you said,

00:12:24.901 --> 00:12:27.730
it's the legacy with everything
that has come before us,

00:12:27.730 --> 00:12:29.310
Fela, my grandfather,

00:12:29.310 --> 00:12:32.310
we've traced ourselves back
to seven generations of music.

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:35.200
It's the plus to the legacy,
is that it still continues.

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:36.450
It doesn't end with us.

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:38.703
It doesn't start or end with either of us.

00:12:38.703 --> 00:12:40.344
- Other musicians outside
of the Kuti family

00:12:40.344 --> 00:12:43.150
have also made waves in Afrobeat music.

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:48.150
Artists like Tony Allen, Ebo
Taylor, Antibalas, Dele Sosimi,

00:12:48.295 --> 00:12:51.840
Lagbaja, Bola Johnson, and KOKOROKO,

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:54.480
a collective of star jazz
musicians from London

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:56.573
creating Afrobeat music.

00:12:56.573 --> 00:12:59.156
(upbeat music)

00:13:02.680 --> 00:13:05.446
Funk's black positive
messages from decades ago

00:13:05.446 --> 00:13:09.120
gave way to the continued
artistic and political awakening

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:10.390
of the United States.

00:13:10.390 --> 00:13:13.980
Specifically, to the global
phenomenon that is hip hop.

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:15.933
Hip hop has a special affinity for Fela,

00:13:15.933 --> 00:13:17.834
name checking him frequently.

00:13:17.834 --> 00:13:20.417
(upbeat music)

00:13:36.126 --> 00:13:39.010
Today you might hear the
term Afrobeats, with an 's',

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:42.420
sometimes referred to as
Afro-pop or Afro-fusion.

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:45.520
Rather than a distinct
genre, Afrobeats is now an

00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:48.660
umbrella term to describe
popular music from West Africa.

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:50.591
What are some things
that some people commonly

00:13:50.591 --> 00:13:54.100
misunderstand about Afrobeat music?

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:56.861
- The difference between
Afrobeat and Afrobeats.

00:13:56.861 --> 00:13:57.694
- Mmm.

00:13:58.550 --> 00:14:02.300
- I think that is the most
common misrepresentation

00:14:04.020 --> 00:14:06.970
because Afrobeats, it's far from,

00:14:06.970 --> 00:14:08.690
very far from the Afrobeat.

00:14:08.690 --> 00:14:12.820
- Afrobeat has more of
a, of a political purpose

00:14:12.820 --> 00:14:13.770
and inspiration.

00:14:13.770 --> 00:14:14.940
- I'd say so. Yeah.

00:14:14.940 --> 00:14:16.097
- I hear that.

00:14:16.097 --> 00:14:19.308
Afrobeats mixes West African
rhythms and harmonic structures

00:14:19.308 --> 00:14:22.110
with the melodies and sleek production

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:23.750
typically found in hip hop.

00:14:23.750 --> 00:14:26.580
Sprinkle in a little extra
influence from black music

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:28.050
from all over the world.

00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:31.720
Techno, soca, dancehall, and trap music.

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:34.270
- I would say more, it
really came out of hip hop,

00:14:34.270 --> 00:14:37.054
but they took one of
the beats of Afrobeat,

00:14:37.054 --> 00:14:40.810
which is they take the snare
drum from the Afrobeat,

00:14:40.810 --> 00:14:42.294
which is a very popular groove of,

00:14:42.294 --> 00:14:44.786
many of my father's tracks, or my track,

00:14:44.786 --> 00:14:49.786
in the Afrobeat has become
the beat of the Afrobeats.

00:14:50.077 --> 00:14:52.420
- Afrobeats is a fusion
of African traditions

00:14:52.420 --> 00:14:54.340
from all across the diaspora.

00:14:54.340 --> 00:14:56.037
You might remember Drake's number one hit,

00:14:56.037 --> 00:14:58.170
"One Dance", from 2016.

00:14:58.170 --> 00:15:01.450
Drake takes influence from
dancehall and Afrobeats,

00:15:01.450 --> 00:15:04.799
and even collaborated with
Nigerian artist, Wizkid.

00:15:04.799 --> 00:15:07.382
(upbeat music)

00:15:08.487 --> 00:15:10.976
"Fem", which means shut up in Yoruba,

00:15:10.976 --> 00:15:13.860
is a song by Nigerian artist, Davido,

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:15.780
that has seen heavy play as an Anthem

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:17.650
for the End SARS movement.

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:20.030
It's just one example of
how the political fervor

00:15:20.030 --> 00:15:22.620
of Fela's original genre is still alive.

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:24.977
Tackling the issues of
the day as they arrive.

00:15:24.977 --> 00:15:27.560
(upbeat music)

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:32.517
Fela Kuti hasn't just
inspired pop stars of Nigeria,

00:15:32.517 --> 00:15:35.170
but pop stars all across the world.

00:15:35.170 --> 00:15:36.958
Beyoncé even paid tribute to Fela

00:15:36.958 --> 00:15:40.197
in her legendary Coachella
performance in 2018.

00:15:40.197 --> 00:15:42.780
(upbeat music)

00:15:47.146 --> 00:15:51.170
Fela's message of love and
pride for his people still rings

00:15:51.170 --> 00:15:54.330
through his life's works
and all that they inspired.

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:58.860
What do you think the future
of Afrobeat music looks like?

00:15:58.860 --> 00:16:03.860
- How Fela has, his music
has really gone further,

00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:07.070
it has ventured much further
than we would have thought

00:16:07.070 --> 00:16:07.990
over the past few years,

00:16:07.990 --> 00:16:09.500
and it seems every year that goes by

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:11.390
he becomes more popular.

00:16:11.390 --> 00:16:14.610
It's a similar kind of
trajectory, I think from, you know

00:16:14.610 --> 00:16:16.610
Western classical music or jazz,

00:16:16.610 --> 00:16:19.640
that the more time goes
by, the bigger it gets,

00:16:19.640 --> 00:16:23.750
and the more, the more all these
sub-genres kind of pop out.

00:16:23.750 --> 00:16:26.921
And it will just grow as a
genre of music, and become

00:16:26.921 --> 00:16:29.093
you know, one of the, the great,

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:32.053
great, you know, top genres.

